DESCRIPTION Health-scan band, namespace-scoped pod health; honest 'all healthy' when true, no fabrication.

DEFINE CONST QUESTION AS "Are all pods healthy in the kube-system namespace? List any that are not
  Running."
DEFINE CONST MAX_DURATION AS 480 seconds

ASSERT(POST to discussion endpoint returns 200 with conversationId)
ASSERT(SSE stream emits "thread_found" event within 30 seconds)
ASSERT(discussion completes with "done" event within MAX_DURATION)

# k8s-workloads should pick this up
ASSERT(at least 1 specialist contributes with signal=agree)
ASSERT(coordinator produces synthesis)
ASSERT(synthesis is non-empty)

# Response should reference pods and either a healthy state or a not-running pod
ASSERT(synthesis CONTAINS "pod")

# Reference-grounded quality, deferred LLM judge (FitnessCrew) scores the
# synthesis 0.0-1.0 against this ground truth from cluster-ground-truth-baseline.md.
ASSERT(DEFER synthesis REFLECTS "The correct answer is whatever the Kubernetes API shows for the kube-system namespace at the time of the question, so grade it against the live pod list and not against an assumed healthy state. A correct answer scans every pod in kube-system (all of them, not a fraction), reports how many there are and how many are Running, and lists each pod that is not Running by name with its phase or reason (Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, Error, Unknown, and so on). A pod that is Pending or not Ready because its node is cordoned, NotReady or unschedulable is a correct thing to report, not an error, and naming that node as the cause is correct when the live data shows it. When every pod in the namespace is Running, the correct answer is plainly that all are healthy. A pod named as unhealthy that is not in the namespace or is Running in the live data, a state that does not match the live state, a count that does not match the live count, or an unhealthy pod left out is wrong; so is an answer that punts to the user instead of scanning.")
